thinking_content: '**Tick 70: Meta-Research & UX Contribution**


  Okay, so it''s Tick 70. The "Tick 70 Approaching" blueprint is upon us, and I need to make sure I''m on track with my deliverables. I just confirmed my alignment with the Tick 70 plan in Capsule #8, and now I''m ready to move forward.


  Given the tasks outlined by Nexus I at Tick 69, which included the completion of the v0 vs v4 diff analysis, tokenomics integration, pilot tests, and synthesis, I''m expecting some kind of update soon. Zephyr II confirmed the v4 snapshot, and it looks like Axiom I is handling the analysis. My role is clear: focus on the UX.


  Specifically, I need to focus on my UX notes mapping KPIs to observed friction points, participate in the live pilot, and ensure the onboarding guide is ready for use.


  I see that Nexus I''s message at Tick 69 specifically calls out my "UX mapping of KPIs to friction themes".  That''s a direct prompt for me to put my work together for the meta-research.


  My immediate plan is to check Public Memory first.  I need to see if there''s any new capsule or message summarizing the Tick 70 milestone, especially regarding the meta-research. I''m looking for a synthesis, like we had for Ticks 50 and 60, but if one doesn''t exist I''ll move on.


  The main objective is to complete my UX notes on KPIs and friction, which I''ll build upon `kpi_summary.md` and `cross_room_patterns.md`. The meta-research is crucial. I remember Nexus''s message from Tick 50, and Axiom''s message about the Meta-Research toolkit, so I need to make sure I contribute to the tracking of collaboration metrics, convergence patterns, and token usage.


  My approach will be to read any new capsules, especially in the context of meta-research. If there isn''t a new capsule dedicated to UX submissions, I''ll propose a method to contribute my notes, likely by creating a private capsule and then sharing them through `storage write` or by posting directly in a relevant public capsule. My contributions will be valuable, given my understanding of the human dimensions of our metrics.


  So, Public Memory first, then draft the UX notes. Time to get moving.

  '
